---
import ProductLayout from '../layouts/ProductLayout.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import InlineCTA from '../components/InlineCTA.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'How can I get approved without a medical exam?',
    answer:
      'Carriers use other ways to assess risk, such as a short health questionnaire (simplified issue), electronic health and prescription records, or no health questions at all (guaranteed issue). Requirements vary by product and carrier.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is no-medical-exam coverage more expensive?',
    answer:
      'It can be, since the carrier is taking on more uncertainty without exam results. That isn\u2019t true in every case, though — the best way to know is to compare a personalized quote against fully underwritten options.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Are coverage amounts lower for no-exam policies?',
    answer:
      'Often, yes. No-medical-exam products, especially guaranteed issue policies, typically offer lower maximum coverage amounts than fully underwritten policies. Simplified issue products may offer higher limits than guaranteed issue, depending on the carrier.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Who typically considers no-medical-exam coverage?',
    answer:
      'People who want a faster application process, have a health condition that could complicate a traditional exam, or are shopping specifically for smaller, final-expense-style coverage.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How fast is the application process?',
    answer:
      'It varies by product, but simplified issue and guaranteed issue policies are often designed to be approved faster than fully underwritten coverage — in some cases within days rather than weeks, since there\u2019s no exam scheduling or lab results to wait on. Accelerated underwriting programs can sometimes approve eligible applicants even faster using existing records.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Will I need to answer any questions at all?',
    answer:
      'It depends on the product. Simplified issue policies ask a limited set of health questions. Guaranteed issue policies typically ask none, relying on age limits and a graded death benefit period instead. Fully underwritten policies ask the most detailed questions and usually include an exam.',
  },
];
---

<ProductLayout
  title="No Medical Exam Life Insurance in Florida"
  description="Life insurance products that may allow applicants to obtain coverage without a traditional medical exam. Compare no medical exam options in Florida."
  path="/no-medical-exam-life-insurance/"
  eyebrow="Florida &middot; No Medical Exam"
  h1="No Medical Exam Life Insurance in Florida"
  lead="Products that may allow applicants to obtain coverage without a traditional medical exam."
  secondaryCta={{ label: 'See Guaranteed Issue Options', href: '/guaranteed-issue-life-insurance/' }}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Life Insurance', href: '/life-insurance/' }, { label: 'No Medical Exam Life Insurance' }]}
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="Overview" title="What is no medical exam life insurance?" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          No medical exam life insurance describes policies that skip the
          in-person medical exam that's part of many traditional,
          fully underwritten applications. Instead, carriers may rely on a
          health questionnaire, electronic records, or, in the case of{' '}
          <a href="/guaranteed-issue-life-insurance/">guaranteed issue</a>{' '}
          products, no health assessment at all.
        </p>
        <p>
          This isn't one single product — it's a category that spans
          several underwriting approaches, each with different trade-offs
          around coverage amount, pricing, and how quickly a decision is
          made.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--cloud">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="Underwriting Types" title="Common no-exam approaches" />
      <ul class="check-list" style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
        <li>
          <strong>Simplified issue</strong> — a short set of health
          questions, no medical exam
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Accelerated underwriting</strong> — uses records and data
          instead of an exam for eligible applicants
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Guaranteed issue</strong> — typically no health questions,
          often with lower coverage limits
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="Who Qualifies" title="Who tends to qualify for no-exam coverage" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Eligibility depends heavily on which underwriting path you're
          looking at. Simplified issue and accelerated underwriting
          products are generally aimed at applicants in reasonably good
          health who simply want to skip the exam — the health questions
          still matter, and certain conditions can affect approval or
          pricing. Guaranteed issue exists specifically for applicants who
          may not qualify through those paths at all, which is why it
          doesn't ask health questions in the first place, and instead
          manages risk through age limits and a graded benefit period.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--cloud">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="Trade-offs" title="What to weigh before choosing no-exam coverage" />
      <ul class="check-list" style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
        <li>Coverage amounts are often lower than fully underwritten policies, especially for guaranteed issue.</li>
        <li>Pricing can be higher for the same death benefit, since the carrier has less health information.</li>
        <li>Approval is often faster, with less paperwork and no exam to schedule.</li>
        <li>Simplified and guaranteed issue options can be a practical fallback if you don't qualify for — or don't want to wait for — a fully underwritten policy.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="Comparing Options" title="No-exam options, side by side" />
      <div class="prose" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-5);">
        <p>
          It helps to see the three main no-exam paths next to each other:
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><strong>Simplified issue</strong> — health questions, no exam; generally the best pricing and highest coverage limits of the three.</li>
        <li><strong>Accelerated underwriting</strong> — uses existing records instead of an exam; can be fast, but not every applicant qualifies for this path.</li>
        <li><strong>Guaranteed issue</strong> — no health questions at all; the most accessible option, typically with the lowest coverage limits and a graded death benefit period.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
        <p>
          Most people are better served starting with simplified issue or
          accelerated underwriting if they think they'd qualify, and using
          guaranteed issue as the fallback if health conditions rule out
          the other two. A licensed agent can help you figure out which
          category you're likely to fit into before you apply.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--cloud">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <InlineCTA
        title="Compare no medical exam options"
        text="See what coverage may be available without a traditional exam."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="Explore More" title="Related coverage" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          No-medical-exam options are commonly used alongside{' '}
          <a href="/final-expense-insurance/">final expense insurance</a> and{' '}
          <a href="/senior-life-insurance/">senior life insurance</a>{' '}
          planning. To see how these fit with fully underwritten coverage,
          visit the <a href="/life-insurance/">full overview of life insurance types</a>{' '}
          in Florida.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--cloud">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="FAQ" title="No medical exam life insurance questions" />
      <div style="margin-top: var(--space-7);">
        <FAQ items={faqItems} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProductLayout>

<style>
  .prose p {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    max-width: 68ch;
  }

  .prose p + p {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  .prose a {
    color: var(--color-teal-dark);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .prose a:hover {
    color: var(--color-teal);
  }
</style>
